# Bidirectional / Trojan-Source control characters in source.
#
# Why this exists: Unicode bidi overrides and isolates (U+202A-202E, U+2066-2069)
# reorder how text is DISPLAYED without changing the bytes a compiler reads. An
# attacker can make a reviewer see one thing (a benign comment, an early return)
# while the code executes another (CVE-2021-42574, "Trojan Source"). These
# characters have no legitimate place in program source, so any occurrence is an
# ERROR.
#
# What it flags: a line containing any of the bidi override/isolate code points.
# The regex matches the LITERAL UTF-8 bytes of those characters (not a \u escape;
# the \u-escaped obfuscation case is a separate concern). Written as a raw RE2
# character class of the actual glyphs.
#
# ERROR / block: display spoofing that hides real behaviour from review.
#
# @see CVE-2021-42574 (Trojan Source), Unicode Technical Report #36.
id: SEC-BIDI-001
title: Bidirectional / Trojan-Source control characters in source
category: security
severity: error
engine: pattern
action: block
patterns:
  - regex: [‪‫‬‭‮⁦⁧⁨⁩]
    message: "Bidirectional/Trojan-Source control character (U+202A-202E / U+2066-2069) — can make code execute differently than it reads to a reviewer. Remove it; these have no legitimate place in source. @see CVE-2021-42574, SEC-BIDI-001"
confidence: 0.9
fix: "Remove the bidirectional control character. If you genuinely need bidi text (e.g. an RTL string literal), isolate it in a resource/data file, never in executable source, and document why."
